"""Chapters 3 and 4."""
from docx.enum.text import WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH as AL

C, L = AL.CENTER, AL.LEFT


def ch3(R):
    R.H1("Chapter 3: Methodology")
    R.H2("3.1 Approach Overview")
    R.P("The study follows six phases, shown in Fig. 3.1. The first phase studies the textbook, the standards and the tool documentation of Chapter 2. The second constructs a small capture file with known timestamps, so that every number that a tool reports can be checked against a value that is known in advance. The third takes a live capture, so that the behaviour of real timestamps is seen as well as the constructed ones. The fourth decodes the files with three independent readers: a parser written for this study that reads the bytes directly, tcpdump, and tshark. The fifth models the effects that matter for correlation, namely time zones, clock offsets, drift and unequal precision, and applies a normalisation procedure to events from several sources. The sixth applies the results to the Target breach.")
    R.FIG("3", "fig_3_1_methodology.png", "Methodology: six phases from study to application")
    R.H2("3.2 Data Collection / Requirements Gathering")
    R.H3("3.2.1 Data sets")
    R.P("Four kinds of data are used, and the report labels each one by its kind, in the same way as the seminar slides. *Constructed* data is a real file built by a script with chosen timestamps. *Live* data is a real capture of real traffic on the loopback interface. *Synthetic* data is generated by a script to follow a documented pattern. *Hypothetical* data is invented to demonstrate a method. *Reported* data is taken from the Senate staff report and the testimony. Table 3.1 lists the data sets and their kind.")
    R.TABLE("3", "Data sets used in the study",
            ["ID", "Data set", "Kind", "Used for"],
            [["D1", "handshake_us.pcap, handshake_ns.pcap, handshake.pcapng: five frames, sample timestamps from the seminar", "Constructed", "File layout, time displays, epoch and precision (Sections 4.1.1–4.1.4)"],
             ["D2", "live_lo.pcap: 16 frames of a TCP exchange on the loopback interface, taken on 18 Nov. 2026", "Live", "Real timestamp behaviour, machine and human gaps (Section 5.1.2)"],
             ["D3", "Five-source event set with different zones, offsets and precision", "Hypothetical", "Normalisation procedure (Sections 4.1.7, 5.1.4)"],
             ["D4", "Firewall and server log lines with a 4 min 10 s offset", "Hypothetical", "Effect of clock error (Section 5.1.3)"],
             ["D5", "Outbound FTP session records, 2–15 Dec. 2013, about 11 GB", "Synthetic", "Time-of-day analysis and zone effect (Section 5.1.6)"],
             ["D6", "Attack and response dates of the Target breach", "Reported", "Timeline (Section 5.1.5)"]],
            [0.4, 2.65, 0.9, 1.82], size=9.5)
    R.H3("3.2.2 Requirements")
    R.P("Before any script was written, the requirements of Table 3.2 were fixed.")
    R.TABLE("3", "Requirements for the study",
            ["ID", "Requirement"],
            [["R1", "Every timestamp quoted in the report must be reproducible from a file or script supplied with the report."],
             ["R2", "Each value must be checked by at least two independent readers (own parser, tcpdump, tshark)."],
             ["R3", "Times must be stored and compared as integers or exact decimals, not as binary floating-point numbers, so that no digits are lost."],
             ["R4", "Every time must be given with its reference (UTC, local zone, or relative) and its precision."],
             ["R5", "Constructed, live, synthetic, hypothetical and reported data must be labelled and never mixed up."],
             ["R6", "Capture only on interfaces and networks that belong to the experimenters; no third-party traffic is captured."]],
            [0.5, 5.27], size=10)
    R.H2("3.3 Architecture")
    R.H3("3.3.1 Capture architecture")
    R.P("Fig. 3.2 shows where the time enters a capture. Traffic flows between a client and a server through a switch. A host on a switch receives only its own frames, so the capture host is attached to a SPAN (mirror) port, a hub or a tap, which gives it a copy of every frame [1]. The capture host runs tcpdump or tshark, which receives frames from the operating system and attaches the reading of the host’s own clock to each one. The clock of the capture host is itself disciplined by an NTP server, and that is the only link to the reference time. In this study the capture host and both endpoints are the same container, which is enough to show timestamp behaviour but not network delay.")
    R.FIG("3", "fig_3_2_architecture.png", "Capture architecture: the capture host stamps each frame with its own clock")
    R.H3("3.3.2 Capture file architecture")
    R.P("Fig. 3.3 shows the classic pcap layout with the real values read from the constructed file. The global header of 24 bytes is followed, for each frame, by a record header of 16 bytes and then the frame bytes. The two timestamp fields, ts_sec and ts_usec, are 32-bit integers; the exact time is their sum, ts_sec + ts_usec / 1,000,000. A file whose magic number is 0xa1b23c4d stores nanoseconds in the second field instead.")
    R.FIG("3", "fig_3_3_pcap_layout.png", "Classic pcap layout with values read from the constructed capture")
    R.H3("3.3.3 Normalisation architecture")
    R.P("Fig. 3.4 shows the five-step procedure used to merge events from several sources. Step 1 records, for every source, its time zone, the measured offset of its clock and its precision. Step 2 converts every time to UTC or to epoch seconds using the recorded zone. Step 3 subtracts the measured clock offset, while the original value is kept as evidence. Step 4 sorts the events by corrected time and notes where precision differs. Step 5 checks that the order makes sense, with causes before effects; if it does not, the clocks are checked again. The order of steps 2 and 3 matters, because zone conversion changes only the display of an instant, whereas offset correction changes the instant itself.")
    R.FIG("3", "fig_3_4_normalisation.png", "Normalisation procedure for events from several sources")
    R.H2("3.4 Implementation Strategy")
    R.P("All experiments run in a Linux container. Table 3.3 lists the tools and their versions. The scripts were written in Python using only the standard library, except the figures, which use matplotlib. Python’s `struct` module is used to build and decode binary records, the `decimal` module to hold exact times, and the `zoneinfo` module, which uses the IANA time zone database, for zone conversion. The scripts are listed in Appendix A and the steps to reproduce every result are in Appendix D.")
    R.TABLE("3", "Tools and environment",
            ["Item", "Version / detail", "Role"],
            [["tcpdump / libpcap", "4.99.4 / 1.10.4", "Live capture; reading files; time options -tt, -ttt, -tttt"],
             ["tshark (Wireshark)", "4.2.2", "Independent decoding; time display formats; frame time fields"],
             ["capinfos, editcap", "Wireshark 4.2.2", "File precision and type; conversion from pcap to pcapng"],
             ["Python", "3.13", "make_pcap.py, parse_pcap.py, gap_analysis.py, timeline_merge.py, drift_model.py, tz_table.py, exfil_window.py"],
             ["matplotlib", "3.11", "Figures (figures.py)"],
             ["Operating system", "Linux 6.18 container, user root", "Capture on the loopback interface requires administrative rights"]],
            [1.5, 1.7, 2.57], size=9.5)


def ch4(R):
    R.H1("Chapter 4: Implementation")
    R.H2("4.1 Execution and Step-by-Step Analysis")
    R.H3("4.1.1 Step 1: construct the capture file")
    R.P("The script make_pcap.py writes five frames, taken from the seminar’s example of a TCP handshake followed by the start of a TLS session, to a classic pcap file. The frames are genuine Ethernet, IPv4 and TCP frames with valid IP and TCP checksums, using the documentation addresses 192.0.2.10 and 198.51.100.7. Only the timestamps in the record headers are chosen: the first is 17:40:01.212754 UTC on 9 November 2026, that is, 1794246001 seconds and 212754 microseconds. Table 4.1 lists the frames. The two payload frames carry placeholder bytes behind a TLS record header, so they stand for a Client Hello and a Server Hello but are not valid TLS messages. A second file with nanosecond precision and a pcapng version were produced from the same frames.")
    R.TABLE("4", "Frames of the constructed capture (kind: constructed)",
            ["No.", "Direction", "TCP segment", "Length (bytes)", "Arrival time (UTC)", "Gap from previous"],
            [["1", "client → server", "SYN", "74", "17:40:01.212754", "0.000000 s"],
             ["2", "server → client", "SYN/ACK", "74", "17:40:01.213981", "0.001227 s"],
             ["3", "client → server", "ACK", "66", "17:40:01.214020", "0.000039 s"],
             ["4", "client → server", "Client Hello (placeholder)", "571", "17:40:01.247566", "0.033546 s"],
             ["5", "server → client", "Server Hello (placeholder)", "1514", "17:40:01.276694", "0.029128 s"]],
            [0.4, 1.1, 1.45, 0.7, 1.2, 0.92], size=9.5, align=[C, L, L, C, C, C])
    R.P("The ladder diagram of Fig. 4.1 shows the same frames in time order. The SYN and SYN/ACK are 1.227 ms apart, the typical reply time of a machine, and the third frame follows after only 39 microseconds.")
    R.FIG("4", "fig_4_1_ladder.png", "Constructed handshake with the arrival time of each frame")
    R.H3("4.1.2 Step 2: read the capture with tcpdump")
    R.P("tcpdump prints the arrival time at the start of every line as hours, minutes, seconds and a fraction of a second. By default it shows only the time of day, in the local zone of the machine, which is UTC in this environment. The options `-tt`, `-ttt` and `-tttt` change the form: raw epoch seconds, the gap since the previous line, and date plus time [5]. The following listing shows the first three frames.")
    R.code("""$ tcpdump -n -r handshake_us.pcap | cut -c1-70
17:40:01.212754 IP 192.0.2.10.51522 > 198.51.100.7.443: Flags [S], seq
17:40:01.213981 IP 198.51.100.7.443 > 192.0.2.10.51522: Flags [S.], se
17:40:01.214020 IP 192.0.2.10.51522 > 198.51.100.7.443: Flags [.], ack
$ tcpdump -n -tt -r handshake_us.pcap | cut -c1-17
1794246001.212754
1794246001.213981
1794246001.214020
$ tcpdump -n -ttt -r handshake_us.pcap | cut -c1-17
 00:00:00.000000
 00:00:00.001227
 00:00:00.000039
$ tcpdump -n -tttt -r handshake_us.pcap | head -1 | cut -c1-26
2026-11-09 17:40:01.212754""")
    R.P("The default time of day omits the date, so for evidence the `-tttt` form, which includes the date, or `-tt`, which gives epoch seconds, should be used. The TCP lines also show the timestamp option (`TS val 1 ecr 0`). Its values 1 and 2 are the sender’s own clock counter and have nothing to do with the arrival times in the first column.")
    R.H3("4.1.3 Step 3: read the capture with tshark and Wireshark")
    R.P("Wireshark’s default packet list shows relative time: the first frame is 0.000000 and every later frame is an offset in seconds from it [6]. This answers how long things took, not at what time they happened. The Frame section of the packet details always keeps the absolute values. For frame 4, tshark reports the following.")
    R.code("""$ tshark -r handshake_us.pcap -Y 'frame.number==4' -V | sed -n '4,10p'
    Arrival Time: Nov  9, 2026 17:40:01.247566000 UTC
    UTC Arrival Time: Nov  9, 2026 17:40:01.247566000 UTC
    Epoch Arrival Time: 1794246001.247566000
    [Time shift for this packet: 0.000000000 seconds]
    [Time delta from previous captured frame: 0.033546000 seconds]
    [Time delta from previous displayed frame: 0.000000000 seconds]
    [Time since reference or first frame: 0.034812000 seconds]""")
    R.P("The View → Time Display Format menu (or the tshark option `-t`) switches the Time column between these representations. Table 4.2 shows one frame, number 4, in the three main formats; they describe the same instant and are tied together by the arithmetic 17:40:01.212754 + 0.034812 = 17:40:01.247566.")
    R.TABLE("4", "One frame in three time display formats (frame 4)",
            ["Display format", "tshark option", "Value shown", "Answers the question"],
            [["Seconds since first captured packet", "-t r", "0.034812", "How long after the start?"],
             ["Date and time of day (UTC)", "-t ud", "2026-11-09 17:40:01.247566", "When, by the clock?"],
             ["Seconds since epoch", "-t e", "1794246001.247566", "Which instant, in a form that sorts and subtracts?"],
             ["Seconds since previous packet", "-t d", "0.033546", "How long after the previous frame?"]],
            [1.9, 0.8, 1.7, 1.37], size=9.5)
    R.H3("4.1.4 Step 4: decode the file bytes")
    R.P("To confirm that the time lives in the file and not in the frame, the first 40 bytes of the file were dumped. Pcap files written on this machine are little-endian, so each 32-bit field is stored with its least significant byte first.")
    R.code("""$ od -A d -t x1 -N 40 handshake_us.pcap
0000000 d4 c3 b2 a1 02 00 04 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
0000016 ff ff 00 00 01 00 00 00 71 05 f2 6a 12 3f 03 00
0000032 4a 00 00 00 4a 00 00 00""")
    R.P("The first 24 bytes are the global header: `d4 c3 b2 a1` is the magic number 0xa1b2c3d4 in little-endian order (microsecond stamps), `02 00 04 00` is version 2.4, `ff ff 00 00` is a snapshot length of 65535 and `01 00 00 00` is link type 1 (Ethernet). The next 16 bytes are the record header of frame 1: `71 05 f2 6a` is ts_sec, `12 3f 03 00` is ts_usec, and the last two fields, `4a 00 00 00` twice, give 74 bytes captured and 74 on the wire.")
    R.P("Reading the bytes `71 05 f2 6a` as a little-endian integer gives 0x6AF20571, which is 1794246001. The bytes `12 3f 03 00` give 0x00033F12, which is 212754. The parser parse_pcap.py repeats this for every record, adds the two parts with exact decimal arithmetic, and prints the result next to the UTC date, the time since the first frame and the gap from the previous frame. Its output agrees with tcpdump and tshark for all five frames. Running it on the nanosecond file shows the magic number 0xa1b23c4d and a fraction field of 212754000; tshark then displays the same instant with nine decimal places. Converting the microsecond file to pcapng with editcap gave a file for which capinfos reports a timestamp precision of microseconds (6): the format can hold other resolutions, but converting does not add precision that the original did not have.")
    R.H3("4.1.5 Step 5: take a live capture")
    R.P("The script live_capture.sh starts tcpdump on the loopback interface, starts a small TCP echo server, and connects a client that sends three messages with pauses of 0.8 s and 1.5 s between them. The pauses imitate a person typing commands. The result is a file of 16 frames. Capturing on loopback needs administrative rights, in line with Messier’s remark that capture requires privileges [1]. Section 5.1.2 analyses this file.")
    R.H3("4.1.6 Step 6: check epoch and time zone conversions")
    R.P("The script tz_table.py converts one instant, 14:30:00 UTC on 9 November 2026, to six zones using the IANA time zone database. The same script confirms that epoch second 1,000,000,000 is 2001-09-09 01:46:40 UTC and that the sample epoch 1794246001 is 2026-11-09 17:40:01 UTC. The epoch is defined as seconds since 00:00:00 UTC on 1 January 1970 [18]; it has no zone and no daylight-saving rule, which is why tools sort and subtract it directly.")
    R.TABLE("4", "One instant, 14:30:00 UTC on 9 November 2026, in six zones (from the IANA database)",
            ["Zone", "Abbreviation", "Offset from UTC", "Local reading"],
            [["UTC", "UTC", "+0:00", "14:30:00, 9 Nov"], ["Europe/London", "GMT", "+0:00", "14:30:00, 9 Nov"],
             ["America/New_York", "EST", "−5:00", "09:30:00, 9 Nov"], ["Asia/Kolkata", "IST", "+5:30", "20:00:00, 9 Nov"],
             ["Asia/Tokyo", "JST", "+9:00", "23:30:00, 9 Nov"], ["Australia/Sydney", "AEDT", "+11:00", "01:30:00, 10 Nov"]],
            [1.7, 1.2, 1.4, 1.47], size=10, align=[L, C, C, C])
    R.H3("4.1.7 Step 7: normalise and merge events from five sources")
    R.P("The script timeline_merge.py implements the procedure of Fig. 3.4 on the hypothetical event set D3. The five sources, with their zones, measured clock offsets and resolutions, are listed in Table 4.4. The firewall logs in Kolkata time with a clock measured four seconds fast; the endpoint antivirus logs in New York time with a clock measured two seconds slow; the packet capture, web server and SIEM log in UTC. The script parses each raw line in the zone of its source, converts it to UTC, subtracts the offset, sorts, and finally validates that every alert is later than the first suspicious packet. Section 5.1.4 reports the result.")
    R.TABLE("4", "Sources in the hypothetical event set (kind: hypothetical)",
            ["Source", "Zone of log", "Measured clock offset", "Resolution"],
            [["Packet capture", "UTC", "none (NTP-synchronised)", "1 µs"], ["Web server", "UTC", "none (NTP-synchronised)", "1 ms"],
             ["Endpoint antivirus", "America/New_York", "2 s slow", "1 s"], ["Firewall", "Asia/Kolkata", "4 s fast", "1 s"],
             ["Monitoring (SIEM)", "UTC", "none (NTP-synchronised)", "1 s"]],
            [1.6, 1.5, 1.7, 0.97], size=10)
    R.H2("4.2 Core Algorithms and Configurations")
    R.H3("4.2.1 Exact timestamp arithmetic")
    R.P("A timestamp is the sum of two integers, but it should not be held in a binary floating-point number. The spacing between consecutive double-precision numbers near 1.794 × 10⁹ is 2.384 × 10⁻⁷ s, about 238 ns, so a microsecond fraction survives but a nanosecond fraction does not: the nanosecond example 1794246001.212754123 turns into 1794246001.212754 when it is converted to a float. The scripts therefore use the `decimal` module (requirement R3).")
    R.code("""# classic pcap: 16-byte little-endian record header -> exact time
ts_sec, ts_frac, incl_len, orig_len = struct.unpack(
    "<IIII", data[off:off+16])
# 10**6 for microsecond files, 10**9 when magic == 0xa1b23c4d
t = Decimal(ts_sec) + Decimal(ts_frac) / Decimal(10**6)
since_first = t - first_frame_time
delta       = t - previous_frame_time""")
    R.H3("4.2.2 Clock drift model")
    R.P("A clock that runs fast by P parts per million accumulates an error of P × 10⁻⁶ seconds for every second that passes. Over one day this is P × 10⁻⁶ × 86,400 s; the time to reach an error E is E / (P × 10⁻⁶). With P = 20 ppm the daily error is 1.728 s, the error after 30 days is 51.84 s, and ten minutes (600 s) are reached after 347 days. The script drift_model.py tabulates these values for 5, 20, 50 and 100 ppm. The value of 20 ppm is used as an illustration of the order of magnitude for an inexpensive quartz oscillator, not as a measurement of any particular machine.")
    R.H3("4.2.3 Offset correction and merge")
    R.P("The core of the normalisation, shown below, parses a local wall-clock string in the zone of its source, converts it to UTC, subtracts the measured offset, and sorts the events. Keeping the raw string next to the corrected time preserves the original evidence.")
    R.code("""# step 1: record - parse the raw line in the zone of its source
local = datetime.strptime(raw, fmt).replace(tzinfo=ZoneInfo(src.tz))
# step 2: convert to UTC
utc = local.astimezone(timezone.utc)
# step 3: correct by the measured clock offset (keep raw for evidence)
corr = utc - timedelta(seconds=src.offset)
events.append((corr, source, message, raw))
# step 4: merge
events.sort(key=lambda e: e[0])
# step 5: validate - no alert may precede the first suspicious packet
valid = all(a >= first_packet for a in alert_times)""")
    R.H3("4.2.4 Gap classification")
    R.P("The script gap_analysis.py reads a capture and classifies the gap between consecutive frames into four bands: below one millisecond (machine speed), one to a hundred milliseconds (network and stack delay), a hundred milliseconds to half a second, and half a second or more (a pause, typically human or application think time). The bands are heuristics for reading a capture, not standards.")
    R.H2("4.3 Challenges Faced & Mitigation")
    R.bullets([
        "**Tools not installed.** tcpdump and tshark were missing in the container. They were installed from the distribution repository so that the values could be checked by independent readers.",
        "**Frame sizes did not match the seminar.** The first version of the constructed frames had a 54-byte ACK instead of 66 bytes because the TCP timestamp option was missing. Adding the 12-byte option to the non-SYN frames and sizing the two payloads fixed the lengths to 66, 571 and 1514 bytes.",
        "**Placeholder payloads are not valid TLS.** Wireshark labels frame 4 as TCP and only frame 5 as TLS. The frames are therefore described as representing a Client Hello and Server Hello, and no claim is made about TLS decoding.",
        "**ICMP could not be captured.** The ping utility is not installed in the container, so the live capture contains TCP traffic only, and the filter was reduced accordingly.",
        "**Floating-point precision.** Epoch seconds with a nanosecond fraction cannot be stored exactly in a double. Exact decimal arithmetic was used throughout.",
        "**Local-time ambiguity.** Local time is ambiguous twice a year when daylight-saving time ends. The experiments use UTC for storage and use zone conversion only for display, in line with Messier’s advice [1].",
        "**No real Target capture.** No packet capture of the breach exists in the public record that was used. The Target chapter therefore uses the reported dates for the timeline and a labelled synthetic data set for the packet-level illustration."])
